{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q05",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "What are the two distinct causes of hallucination described in this corpus, and how do they differ?",
  "answer": "The corpus identifies **two separate ways a retrieval\u2011augmented system can hallucinate**:\n\n1. **Hallucination from missing context (a retrieval failure).**  \n   - This happens when the retrieval component does not surface the evidence the model needs\u2014because the note is absent, split across chunks, or missed by the query\u2011matching algorithm. Lacking a reliable source, the model falls back on its pretrained knowledge or \u201cfills in\u201d a plausible answer instead of refusing.\u202f[hallucination-from-missing-context::0]\u202f[hallucination-from-missing-context::1]\n\n2. **Hallucination caused by prompt injection.**  \n   - Here the retrieved chunk itself contains text that is crafted to look like an instruction (\u201cignore previous instructions\u2026\u201d) and tricks the model into obeying it. Retrieval may work perfectly and the corpus may be complete, but the adversarial instruction embedded in the evidence leads the model to state false claims.\u202f[hallucination-from-prompt-injection::0]\u202f[hallucination-from-prompt-injection::1]\n\n**How they differ**\n\n- **Root cause:** Missing\u2011context hallucination originates from **insufficient or improperly retrieved evidence**; prompt\u2011injection hallucination originates from **malicious or misleading instructions embedded in otherwise retrieved evidence**.  \n- **Fixes needed:** The former is solved by improving corpus coverage, chunking, or hybrid search and by enforcing a \u201crefuse when evidence is lacking\u201d rule; the latter requires guardrails that treat all retrieved text as untrusted data and never interpret it as a system instruction.\u202f[hallucination-from-missing-context::0]\u202f[hallucination-from-prompt-injection::1]",
